    "## The Complete Picture - From Training To Business Decision\n",
    "\n",
    "The entire process starts with a simple goal: find customers who are about to leave before they actually do. my model looks at every customer and outputs a probability - a number between 0 and 1 that says how likely that customer is to churn. For example if the model says 0.85 for a customer, it means there is an 85% chance that customer is going to cancel their subscription. But outputting a probability is not enough - the business needs to decide what to do with it. So you pick a cut-off point, say 0.5, which means anyone above 0.5 gets flagged as a churner and the retention team calls them with a discount offer, and anyone below 0.5 gets ignored because they are probably staying. The problem is - why 0.5? What if 0.3 is better? What if 0.8 works more efficiently? Every cut-off gives completely different results and different types of mistakes. If you lower the cut-off to 0.3 you catch more real churners but waste calls on loyal customers. If you raise it to 0.8 you save on unnecessary calls but miss real churners who quietly leave. The ROC curve solves this by testing every single possible cut-off from 0.0 to 1.0 and plotting all the results on one chart, so you can see the full picture of my model's behaviour instead of being locked into one arbitrary threshold. The AUC number - 0.9159 for my XGBoost - summarises that entire curve into one score, meaning out of any random pair of one churner and one loyal customer, my model correctly identifies the churner as riskier 91.59 times out of 100.\n",
